course: space
lesson: 32
title: Olympiad: stars, light and space history ★★★
level: primary-4
stars: 3
explain: The second olympiad lesson leaves the planets for the stars, starlight and the people who explored space: each puzzle joins a distance, a colour, a star's life or a date to another fact.
explain: The biggest trap is the light year: it is a distance, how far light goes in a year, so light from a star 4 light years away left it 4 years ago, and you see the star as it was then, not as it is now.
explain: A star's colour is a thermometer: red stars are the coolest, yellow ones like the Sun sit in the middle and blue-white ones are the hottest - the opposite of the red and blue taps on a sink.
explain: Picture Finn from Glasgow on a dreich night (dull and drizzly, in Scots), saying "That wee star's been up there a million years, ken" ("ken" means you know) - the million is hyperbole, but the real puzzle is how long its light has been travelling.
explain: Dates can be put in order like a story: the first rocket to reach space, the first satellite, the first animal in orbit, the first person, the first Moon landing and the space station that has never been empty since 2000.
fact: A light year is a distance, not a time: it is how far light travels in one year, about 9.5 trillion kilometres.
fact: Proxima Centauri is about 4.2 light years away, Sirius about 8.6, Vega about 25 and Polaris about 430, so their light left them that many years ago.
fact: Sunlight takes about 8 minutes 20 seconds to reach the Earth and moonlight about 1.3 seconds.
fact: Red stars are the coolest, about 3,000 degrees Celsius, the yellow-white Sun is about 5,500 degrees, and blue-white stars can be over 20,000 degrees.
fact: In Orion, Betelgeuse is a red star and Rigel is a blue-white star, so Rigel's surface is much hotter.
fact: A Sun-like star swells into a red giant, puffs off a planetary nebula and ends as a white dwarf about the size of the Earth.
fact: Stars more than about 8 times as heavy as the Sun end in a supernova and leave a neutron star, about 20 kilometres across, or a black hole.
fact: Stars twinkle because moving air bends their light; above the air, as on the International Space Station, they shine steadily.
fact: Sirius is the brightest star in the night sky; Polaris, the Pole Star, is not, but it stays almost still above due north and is found with the Pointers of the Plough.
fact: The Moon is moving away from the Earth by about 3.8 centimetres a year.
fact: In order: a V-2 rocket reached space in 1944, Sputnik 1 orbited in October 1957, Laika in November 1957, Gagarin flew in 1961, Tereshkova in 1963, Apollo 11 landed in 1969, the Voyagers launched in 1977 and the space station has had people since 2000.
fact: Helium was found in the Sun's light in 1868, more than 25 years before it was found on the Earth, and its name comes from the Greek word for Sun.
fact: Around 240 BC Eratosthenes estimated the size of the Earth by comparing the Sun's shadows at two cities in Egypt.
fact: Even at Voyager's speed, a trip to Proxima Centauri would take about 75,000 years.
exercise: Proxima Centauri is 4.2 light years away and Mo is 10. About how old was he when tonight's Proxima light set off? | About 6 | About 4 | About 14 | About 10
exercise: Which pair are both units of distance? | A light year and an astronomical unit | A light year and a sol on Mars | A sol and an Earth day | An astronomical unit and a month
exercise: Put these star colours in order, coolest first: blue-white, red, yellow. | Red, yellow, blue-white | Blue-white, yellow, red | Yellow, red, blue-white | Red, blue-white, yellow
exercise: Betelgeuse is red and Rigel is blue-white. Which has the hotter surface? | Rigel | Betelgeuse | Both the same | Colour cannot tell us
exercise: Put the Sun's far future in order. | Red giant, planetary nebula, white dwarf | White dwarf, red giant, planetary nebula | Planetary nebula, red giant, white dwarf | Red giant, white dwarf, planetary nebula
exercise: A star 20 times as heavy as the Sun dies. What is left? | A neutron star or a black hole | A white dwarf about the size of the Earth | A red dwarf, cool, small and dim | A new planet made of its ashes
exercise: If the Sun suddenly went dark, how long until we on the Earth would notice? | About 8 minutes 20 seconds | No time at all, at once | About 1.3 seconds | About 4.2 years
exercise: Which is brighter in our night sky, and which stays still above due north? | Sirius is brighter; Polaris stays still | Polaris is brighter and stays still | Sirius is brighter and stays still | Polaris is brighter; Sirius stays still
exercise: Put these in order: Gagarin's flight, Sputnik 1, Apollo 11, Laika. | Sputnik 1, Laika, Gagarin, Apollo 11 | Laika, Sputnik 1, Gagarin, Apollo 11 | Gagarin, Sputnik 1, Laika, Apollo 11 | Sputnik 1, Gagarin, Laika, Apollo 11
exercise: How many years passed between the first satellite and the first Moon landing? | 12 | 8 | 25 | 16
exercise: The Moon drifts about 3.8 centimetres a year further away. About how far in 100 years? | About 3.8 metres | About 38 centimetres | About 38 metres | About 3.8 kilometres
exercise: Which gas was spotted in the Sun's light before anyone found it on the Earth? | Helium | Hydrogen | Oxygen | Carbon dioxide
exercise: An astronaut on the space station looks out at the stars. What is different? | They shine steadily, without twinkling | They twinkle far more than at home | They all look exactly the same colour | They cannot be seen at all from orbit
exercise: Sirius's light reaches a 9-year-old tonight. When did it set off? | When she was a baby, under 1 | Long before she was born | Last night, just before dark | When she was about 8 and a half
exercise: Which is wider, a white dwarf or a neutron star? | A white dwarf, about the Earth's size | A neutron star, about the Earth's size | Neither: both about 20 km across | A neutron star, wider than the Sun
exercise: What did Eratosthenes compare to estimate the size of the Earth? | The Sun's shadows at two cities | Two pictures taken from a high tower | The Moon's phases on two nights | The stars seen from two ships
test: ★★★ Finn from Glasgow, on a dreich night: "Took me light years to finish my homework, ken." What is wrong with that, besides the joke? | A light year is a distance, not a length of time | Nothing: a light year is just a very long time | A light year is only one day, so it is too short | It should be "light months", which is a time
test: ★★★ Priya: "Vega's light left it 25 years ago, so if it changed last week we'd still see the old Vega." Check the claim. | True: we see Vega as it was 25 years ago | False: we always see stars as they are now | False: Vega's light gets here in 8 minutes | True, but only for red stars, not blue ones
test: ★★★ Tyler from Ohio: "Red's hot and blue's cold, like the taps, no cap." What is true for stars? | Blue-white stars are hottest, red coolest | He is right: it works just like the taps | Every star is the same heat, colour is dust | Yellow stars like the Sun are the hottest
test: ★★★ Gran: "The Pole Star's the brightest star in the sky, that's how sailors found it." What is right? | Sirius is brighter; Polaris is found by the Pointers | She is right: the Pole Star is the brightest | Polaris is the brightest, but it moves about | The Pole Star is really the planet Venus
test: ★★★ Jack, rough: "Shut up, the Sun's gonna go supernova and we're all dead." What will a Sun-like star really do? | Become a red giant, then a white dwarf | Explode as a supernova, just as he says | Turn into a black hole in a year or two | Shrink and cool down into a giant planet
test: ★★★ Amara says, "Gagarin went first, then the satellite, then the dog." Put it right. | Sputnik 1, then Laika, then Gagarin | Laika, then Sputnik 1, then Gagarin | Gagarin, then Laika, then Sputnik 1 | She is right; that was the order
test: ★★★ Mum: "The Moon's moving off by 3.8 centimetres a year, nothing really." About how far in 1,000 years? | About 38 metres | About 3.8 metres | About 380 metres | About 3.8 kilometres
test: ★★★ Liam from Dublin: "Sure Voyager'd get to Proxima before me nan's birthday, it's grand." How long would it really take? | About 75,000 years | About 4.2 years | About 47 years | About 8 minutes
test: ★★★ An astronaut texts from the space station: "stars r not twinkling up here lol, broken??" Best answer? | Not broken: twinkling comes from air, and you're above it | Yes: stars switch off their twinkle in space | The window glass stops them from blinking | Stars twinkle only on clear summer nights
test: ★★★ Zara from Bristol: "Helium were found on Earth first, then they spotted it in the Sun, proper job." Check the claim. | False: it was seen in the Sun first, in 1868 | True: it was found in party balloons first | False: it was first found on the Moon | True, and the name means "from Earth"
test: ★★★ Kofi: "Apollo 11 was 1969 and the station's had people since 2000, so that's about 21 years apart." What is the real gap? | 31 years | 21 years | 41 years | 29 years
test: ★★★ "I've told you a million times: Sirius is 8.6 light years away and shines 25 times brighter than the Sun!" Which number is hyperbole? | A million times | 8.6 light years | 25 times | None of them
exam: State the approximate age of a ten-year-old observer at the moment when the light of Proxima Centauri now reaching him departed that star. | About six years | About four years | About fourteen years | About ten years
exam: Arrange the following stellar colours in order of increasing surface temperature: blue-white, red, yellow. | Red, yellow, blue-white | Blue-white, yellow, red | Yellow, red, blue-white | Red, blue-white, yellow
exam: Identify the earliest of the following events. | The launch of Sputnik 1 | The orbital flight of Laika | The flight of Yuri Gagarin | The landing of Apollo 11
exam: Explain why stars observed from the International Space Station do not twinkle. | Their light does not pass through moving air | The station's windows filter starlight | The stars are much nearer to the station | The station spins too fast for twinkling
